{"instructions": "Explain how a university registration system should handle two students simultaneously requesting the last seat. Discuss transactions, duplicate prevention, and the benefits and risks of caching. This is a theoretical answer, not a coding task.", "criterion": "Concrete protection against overbooking", "rubric": {"policy": "Assess theoretical understanding. A valid conceptual explanation suffices; do not require SQL code, every listed alternative, or topics outside the selected criterion. Separate independent criteria. An explicitly false claim is incorrect, not just missing coverage.", "criterion": "Concrete protection against overbooking", "professional_principle": "A correct reservation must atomically check and consume shared remaining capacity under concurrent requests. Plain transaction grouping or per-student uniqueness alone does not make the capacity check safe. Separate reads followed by updates can race unless protected.", "valid_solutions": ["Within the enrollment transaction, a conditional capacity update succeeds only while remaining seats are positive; accept enrollment only when it succeeds, and roll back on later failure.", "Hold an exclusive lock on the shared course-capacity record inside the transaction, then recheck capacity, consume a seat and create enrollment before commit.", "Serializable processing with correct handling/retry of serialization conflicts, or an equivalent explicitly explained atomic capacity reservation."], "scope": "Any one sound approach is sufficient. Do not demand locks if a valid conditional update is given, or vice versa. A cache availability check, an unprotected database recheck, an ordinary transaction or a per-student UNIQUE key does not alone prove safe shared-capacity reservation."}, "submission": "I would add a UNIQUE constraint on student ID and course ID. This guarantees that two different students cannot take the last seat. A transaction can update the seat count and save the enrollment together, with rollback if either fails. A cache makes reads faster. If the cache says a seat is available, we must check the database before confirming enrollment because cached availability may be stale."}
